### How does interface projection support SpEL computed fields?

Interface projections support `@Value` with SpEL expressions to compute fields at runtime. This is something DTO projections cannot do.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;

public interface AccountSummary {
    String getAccountId();
    String getHolderName();
    BigDecimal getBalance();

    @Value("#{target.holderName + ' (' + target.accountId + ')'}")
    String getDisplayName();
    // Computed at runtime: "Rajesh Kumar (SAV2001)"
    // SpEL evaluates against the target entity
    // SQL only fetches: account_id, holder_name, balance
}
```

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.dto.AccountSummary;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    List<AccountSummary> findByHolderNameContaining(String name);
    // SQL: SELECT account_id, holder_name, balance
    //      FROM accounts WHERE holder_name LIKE '%name%'
    // getDisplayName() computed by SpEL at runtime
}
```

### How do nested interface projections work?

Interfaces can declare nested interfaces for association projections. Spring handles the JOIN and mapping automatically.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountWithTradesView {
    String getAccountId();
    String getHolderName();
    BigDecimal getBalance();
    List<TradeView> getTrades();  // nested projection!

    interface TradeView {
        String getTradeId();
        String getSymbol();
        BigDecimal getAmount();
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.dto.AccountWithTradesView;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import java.util.Optional;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    Optional<AccountWithTradesView> findByAccountId(String accountId);
    // Returns account with nested trade projections
    // Only selected fields fetched from both tables
}
```

### Why can DTO constructor expressions not handle nested collections?

JPQL constructor expressions create objects with scalar values. They cannot map a collection (like `List<Trade>`) into a constructor parameter. For nested data, use interface projections or build the DTO manually in the service layer.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// DTO record: works for flat data
public record TradeOverview(String tradeId, String symbol, BigDecimal amount) {}

// This DOES NOT work as a constructor expression:
// public record AccountWithTrades(
//     String accountId,
//     String holderName,
//     List<TradeOverview> trades  // cannot map collection in JPQL constructor!
// ) {}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.TradeOverview;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class AccountDetailService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountDetailService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // Build nested DTO manually in service layer
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public AccountDetailDTO getDetail(String accountId) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findWithTrades(accountId).orElseThrow();
        List<TradeOverview> trades = account.getTrades().stream()
            .map(t -> new TradeOverview(t.getTradeId(), t.getSymbol(), t.getAmount()))
            .toList();
        return new AccountDetailDTO(
            account.getAccountId(),
            account.getHolderName(),
            account.getBalance(),
            trades
        );
    }
}
```

### How does dynamic projection let the caller choose?

Dynamic projection uses a `Class<T>` parameter so the same repository method returns different projections based on what the caller requests.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    <T> List<T> findByHolderNameContaining(String name, Class<T> type);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.AccountSummary;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class AccountSearchService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountSearchService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public void dynamicDemo() {
        // Same method, different projections:
        List<AccountSummary> summaries =
            accountRepo.findByHolderNameContaining("Rajesh", AccountSummary.class);
        // Only 3 columns + computed displayName

        List<Account> fullEntities =
            accountRepo.findByHolderNameContaining("Rajesh", Account.class);
        // All columns, full entity in persistence context
    }
}
```

### Why do projections beat full entity loading?

Projections reduce three costs: network (fewer columns transferred), memory (no persistence context), and CPU (no dirty checking).

```text
20-column entity, 10,000 rows, API needs 3 columns:

  Full entity:
    Data: 20 cols x 10K = 200K field reads
    Memory: ~5MB data + ~5MB snapshots = 10MB
    CPU: 200K dirty checking comparisons at flush
    Network: 500 bytes/row x 10K = 5MB from DB

  DTO projection (3 columns):
    Data: 3 cols x 10K = 30K field reads
    Memory: ~300KB (plain records)
    CPU: zero dirty checking
    Network: 75 bytes/row x 10K = 750KB from DB

  Savings: 85% less data, 97% less memory, zero dirty checking
```

### How is interface projection different from DTO projection?

| Feature | Interface | DTO (Record/Class) |
|---------|----------|--------------------|
| SpEL @Value | Supported | Not supported |
| Nested projections | Supported | Not in constructor expression |
| Implementation | Spring proxy | Direct instantiation |
| Performance | Proxy overhead | No overhead (fastest) |
| Definition | Interface with getters | Record with constructor |
| Use case | Computed fields, nesting | Production API responses |

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;

// Interface: supports SpEL and nesting
public interface AccountSummary {
    String getAccountId();
    String getHolderName();
    @Value("#{target.holderName + ' (' + target.accountId + ')'}")
    String getDisplayName();  // computed!
}

// DTO record: fastest, no proxy
// public record AccountOverview(String accountId, String holderName) {}
// No SpEL support, no nesting
```

### What are common mistakes with projections?

The most common mistake is returning full entities from read-only API endpoints when a projection would be more efficient.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.dto.TradeOverview;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
public class TradeController {

    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;

    public TradeController(TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    // WRONG: returning full entity for a list API
    // @GetMapping("/trades")
    // public List<Trade> getTrades() {
    //     return tradeRepo.findByStatus("SETTLED");
    //     // All 20 columns loaded, 10MB for 10K rows
    // }

    // CORRECT: return DTO projection
    @GetMapping("/trades")
    public List<TradeOverview> getTrades() {
        return tradeRepo.findOverviewByStatus("SETTLED");
        // Only 3 columns loaded, 300KB for 10K rows
    }
}
```

### When should you use each projection form?

Use DTO projections for production API responses (fastest). Use interface projections when you need SpEL computed fields or nested association projections. Use dynamic projections when the same method needs to return different shapes. Use full entities only when you need to modify the data.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.AccountSummary;
import com.bank.dto.TradeOverview;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class ProjectionDecisionService {

    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;

    public ProjectionDecisionService(TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    // READ API: DTO projection (fastest)
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<TradeOverview> listTrades(String status) {
        return tradeRepo.findOverviewByStatus(status);
    }

    // WRITE: full entity (dirty checking needed)
    @Transactional
    public void settleTrade(String tradeId) {
        Trade trade = tradeRepo.findById(tradeId).orElseThrow();
        trade.setStatus("SETTLED");
    }
}
```
